WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:06.740
For many people, the history of space exploration
often feels like a very American story.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:12.420
And while the moon landing is rightly placed
as the shiny gold medal, the Russian cosmonaut

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:19.050
program was incredibly successful and achieved
many firsts that are too often overlooked;

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.230
Moscow, we have a problem.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:26.260
These days, space exploration is a much more
collaborative process and is often controlled

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.949
by private businesses rather than governments.

00:00:28.949 --> 00:00:32.960
Although perhaps Elon Musk is building an
evil moon base and will become our cosmic

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.079
overlord.

00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:39.150
So today we’re looking at some of the biggest
contributions Russia made to mankind’s exploration

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:44.829
of space.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:50.809
The founding father of rocketry and aeronautics
was Konstantin Tsiolkovsky, a partially deaf

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:55.390
scientist who was home-schooled and ended
up living as a recluse in a log cabin south

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.539
of Moscow.

00:00:56.539 --> 00:01:01.280
He believed that life in space was the future
of mankind, where we could live forever and

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.049
be free from all our worries, sort of like
a floating Disneyland.

00:01:05.049 --> 00:01:09.941
He seemingly thought about every aspect of
space travel; from steering thrusters and

00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:15.170
a multi-stage booster system to airlocks and
even the closed system of biology you would

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.060
need to make self-sustaining food systems.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.430
In 1903 he published Exploration of Outer
Space by Means of Rocket Devices and in it

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:29.180
he calculated that you would need a speed
of 8000m/s in order to hold a minimal earth

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:30.180
orbit.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.259
He showed how this could be achieved with
a multi-stage rocket fueled by liquid oxygen

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:39.929
and liquid hydrogen, using an earlier formula
he had created to show how the mass lost though

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.439
burning fuel affects a body’s acceleration.

00:01:43.439 --> 00:01:48.460
He was the first person to show that space
travel was actually possible, rather than

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.030
just a Jules Verne fantasy.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.930
Not sure the same will happen with the whole
Journey to the Centre of the Earth thing though,

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.799
Jules, it’s bloody hot down there.

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:03.399
Tsiolkovsky inspired many others, including
the German, Hermann Oberth, and American,

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:07.200
Robert H. Goddard, who built the first liquid
fueled rocket.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.440
But within Russia, Sergei Korolev was his
greatest disciple and went on to lead the

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.600
Soviet Space program.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:21.940
Korolev started out as an aircraft designer
but in 1938 he was arrested as part of Stalin’s

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:26.440
great purge, where Stalin cleared out the
communist party of anyone who might oppose

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.440
him.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:32.750
By the time Korolev’s appeal was awarded,
he had already spent months in a Siberian

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:35.960
gulag, starving to death down a mine.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:40.500
He lost many of his teeth thanks to scurvy
and just generally had a really great time.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:45.330
His death sentence was overturned and he spent
5 years in a Sharashka, which was basically

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:50.790
a prison for brainboxes so they could be punished
but the country could still benefit from their

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:51.790
expertise.

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.310
It’s similar to how many university professors
feel today.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.810
He began work on rocket boosters, because
apparently just escaping prison with a ladder

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.600
would not have been good enough for him.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.000
After World War 2, he worked on trying to
understand and improve the German V2 missile

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:14.570
design and eventually, in 1957, the R-7 Semyorka
successfully launched, the world’s first

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:18.040
true intercontinental ballistic missile.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:22.620
On the back of this success, he persuaded
Khrushchev, then leader of Russia, to allow

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.620
him to launch a satellite and beat the Americans
to the punch.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.980
In just a month they cobbled together a metal
sphere with a transmitter, a few instruments

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:37.340
and batteries, and on 4 October 1957, Sputnik
became the first ever satellite, launched

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.140
on a modified R-7 rocket.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:45.460
Sputnik is the Russian for satellite but was
previously used to mean “travelling companion”.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:50.870
Sputnik 2 contained the dog Laika, the first
animal in space, who died of heat exhaustion

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.310
in just hours.

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:57.820
Sputnik 3 launched but its instruments failed,
which were intended to map the Van Allen radiation

00:03:57.820 --> 00:03:58.820
belts.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.470
The west continued to obliviously label every
subsequent Russian satellite or space station

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:09.690
as Sputnik 4, 5, 6 etc. even though in Russia
they had proper names for them.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:14.830
Belka and Strelka were the first animals to
successfully return from space and you can

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:19.340
actually visit their little stuffed bodies
in the Memorial Museum of Cosmonautics, in

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:20.340
Moscow.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:24.630
Around 50 dogs were used in the program, mainly
mongrel mutts as they were more resilient

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.380
and predominantly female ones as they were
better behaved.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:34.530
The final test flight was in the Vostok 3KA-2,
when a dog named Zvezdochka along with a human

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:40.350
sized dummy named Ivan Ivanovich, returned
from a low earth orbit.

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:47.150
Korolev really wanted to follow Tsiolkovsky’s
big ambition and head straight for Mars but

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:52.470
the Space Race with the Americans meant that
politics decided what the aims should be,

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.300
so the Moon became the target.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:05:00.340
The rate of achievement was phenomenal, as
both sides pushed to achieve a series of firsts.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.650
There was barely any testing and deadlines
were suddenly dragged forward as news from

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.810
the other side came in.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:12.190
Luna 2 was the first space probe to land on
Mars, after they missed by 6000 km with Luna

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.020
1, everyone needs a warm up shot, I guess.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:20.190
Luna 3 gave us the first shots of the far
side of the moon but it was 14 years before

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:23.530
Pink Floyd released the soundtrack, so they
were a bit too early.

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:32.190
And then on 12 April 1961, the big one, the
first human to leave the planet; Yuri Gagarin.

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:37.610
The whole trip in the Vostock 1 took just
108 minutes, including the time it took him

00:05:37.610 --> 00:05:40.470
to parachute down from 7 kilometers up.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:45.110
But even for such a short trip, Gagarin stopped
the bus on the way to the rocket to take a

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:50.240
quick, preparatory leak, a tradition which
is now continued on every Russian mission;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.300
a Urine Gagarin, if you will.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:57.720
And the Russians sent empty bladders up on
a pretty regular basis from then on, as they

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:04.270
crossed off many other firsts such as Alexey
Leonov’s spacewalk and Valentina Tereshkova

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:11.090
flying the female flag in space, beating the
American Sally Ride by almost exactly 20 years.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:16.860
Interestingly, Sally is still the youngest
American to leave earth, at 32 but Gagarin

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:23.670
was just 27 and Gherman Titov was only 25,
the first to spend a day in space.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.910
And what about the future?

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:31.140
Well there are a range of orbiters and landers
being sent up to the moon but this seems to

00:06:31.140 --> 00:06:32.750
just be a bit of warm up.

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:38.140
The exciting things in the pipeline are various
collaborations with NASA, yes that’s right,

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:40.560
Russia is collaborating with NASA.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:46.040
The Kremlin also put 1.5 million dollars into
planning a mission to Ganymede, Jupiter’s

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.240
largest moon.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:51.980
Of course, we all know the big secret that
the Russian space program have been hiding

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:58.000
from us; that if you open up a Russian rocket,
you find another slightly smaller rocket hiding

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:24.030
inside, and then another smaller rocket, it’s
a very clever system.

